I have good references now:

1. **LearnOpenGL — Collision resolution (Breakout)**: https://learnopengl.com/In-Practice/2D-Game/Collisions/Collision-resolution — the canonical reference: "Whenever a collision occurs we want two things to happen: we want to reposition the ball so it is no longer inside the other object and second, we want to change the direction of the ball's velocity" — flip the axis of the hit face (left/right → vx, top/bottom → vy), reposition by penetration. This is the resolution of reference for D1/D4/D5.

2. **Hamaluik — Swept AABB using Minkowski difference**: https://blog.hamaluik.ca/posts/swept-aabb-collision-using-minkowski-difference/ — canonical for tunneling: "Continuous collision detection is essential in any game where you have fast-moving objects and/or low frame rates... prevent what is called tunneling" — if velocity*dt > collider size, discrete sampling misses. Also notes the degenerate case: swept doesn't work if already colliding → resolve discrete first. For D2.

